Letter Partners Game

The first thirteen letters of the English alphabet are pre-partners and the last thirteen are their matching post-partners: `a` pairs with `n`, `b` with `o`, through `m` with `z`.

A word wins when every pre-partner has its matching post-partner later in the word. If pairs are not adjacent, they must close in nesting order: a later pre-partner’s post-partner closes before an earlier pre-partner’s post-partner.

For example, `abon` wins because `b`/`o` closes before `a`/`n`. `abno` loses because `a`/`n` closes before `b`/`o`. `aerfsbon` also wins.
